    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Можно тебя на минуточку?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can I have a word with you for a minute?' Notice how 'may' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'a minute'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am listening.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'I'm listening'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Ты много сделала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You have done a lot.' Kristina links 'you' directly with 'a lot' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Но это прошлое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But this is the past.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'but' directly with 'last'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Нам нужны новые люди.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need new people.' Notice how 'us' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'people'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Новая кровь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'New blood.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'new' with 'blood'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы меня увольняете?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you firing me?' Notice how 'you' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'are firing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Да.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'yes' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я надеюсь, ты найдешь работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hope you will find a job.' Kristina links 'I' directly with 'hope so' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не волнуйтесь за меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not worry about me.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'worry' turns the action back onto the subject. And Kristina jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Хорошо, не буду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Okay, I won't.' Kristina links 'good' directly with 'not' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "До свидания.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
